---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import Diagram from '@/components/Diagram.astro';
---

<Layout title="How to read the model" description="A practical guide to identifiers, resources, classes, properties, SKOS concepts, SHACL shapes and provenance in SPDTF.">
  <h1>How to read the model</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Read an ontology as a set of precise sentences about identified things. Boxes usually
    show resources or classes; labelled lines show relationships; tables show literal
    values and constraints that would make a diagram unreadable.
  </p>

  <h2 id="identifiers">Identifiers and resources</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>resource</strong> is anything the graph identifies: a real-world thing, a
    concept, a relationship, a document, a vocabulary scheme or the ontology itself. An
    Internationalised Resource Identifier (IRI) gives it a stable reference. Human labels
    can change or vary by language without changing that identity.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Identity is separate from display text</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Model element</th><th scope="col">Example</th><th scope="col">What to ask</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">IRI</th><td><code>…/Property</code></td><td>Is this identifier stable, owned and versioned?</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Preferred label</th><td>Property</td><td>Is this the clearest term for the intended audience?</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Definition</th><td>The agreed description for this context.</td><td>Can a practitioner tell what is included and excluded?</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Provenance</th><td>Source document, participant evidence or decision.</td><td>Why was this meaning proposed, and who reviewed it?</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="classes-properties">Classes, properties and values</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>class</strong> describes a kind of thing, such as <em>Property</em> or
    <em>Legal title</em>. An individual resource may be described as a member of a class.
    An <strong>object property</strong> links one resource to another; a
    <strong>datatype property</strong> links a resource to a literal value such as text,
    a date, a number or a boolean.
  </p>
  <p>
    RDFS domain and range statements describe semantic implications: using a property may
    imply what kind of thing its subject and object are. They are not form-validation
    rules. SHACL shapes express the validation expectations for a declared use.
  </p>
  <Diagram size="large" caption="The same example seen through the main modelling constructs. Class-to-class and class-to-concept links form the business topology. Literal values stay in detail tables; the SKOS choice, SHACL constraint, provenance evidence and candidate version are shown as attached record nodes to explain their roles.">
flowchart LR
  accTitle: The building blocks of an SPDTF ontology record
  accDescr: A property class connects to address and title classes; a value scheme supplies a status concept, SHACL describes expectations, and provenance records support the candidate.
  Property["Class: Property"]:::service
  Address["Class: Address"]:::infra
  Title["Class: Legal title"]:::security
  Status["SKOS concept: status"]:::data
  Shape["SHACL shape"]:::process
  Evidence["Evidence record"]:::external
  Candidate["Candidate version"]:::user
  Property -->|has address| Address
  Title -->|registered over| Property
  Property -->|has status| Status
  Shape -.->|checks expected data| Property
  Evidence -.->|supports| Candidate
  Candidate -->|defines| Property
  </Diagram>

  <h2 id="vocabularies">Controlled values and taxonomies</h2>
  <p>
    A status such as “listed”, “under offer” or “withdrawn” is normally a governed
    <strong>SKOS concept</strong> in a concept scheme, not an OWL class. SKOS provides
    preferred and alternative labels, definitions, codes and scheme membership. Within a
    scheme, broader/narrower can express taxonomy; across schemes, reviewed SKOS concepts
    may use mapping predicates. Those predicates do not map OWL classes or properties.
    See the <a href="/semantic-modelling/evidence-and-mappings#cross-context-mappings">Category 8 mapping guide</a>.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Choose the construct that matches the business meaning</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Likely construct</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Is this a reviewed kind whose instances share the same intended meaning?</th><td>OWL class</td><td>Property, organisation, legal title, or a context-dependent role</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Is this one governed choice from a value set?</th><td>SKOS concept</td><td>Listing status, tenure category</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Is this text, a date or a number?</th><td>Datatype property and literal</td><td>Display address, date recorded</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Does the relationship itself need evidence, dates or roles?</th><td>Qualified relationship resource</td><td>A party acting in a role for a transaction</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Instance identity and class selection are separate decisions. A person or property
    may have stable identity, while a role such as buyer is temporary and context-dependent
    (an ontology term for this is <em>anti-rigid</em>): it can stop applying without the
    person ceasing to exist. That role can still be represented as an OWL class when the
    reviewed model needs to classify participation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="shapes-provenance">Shapes and provenance</h2>
  <p>
    An ontology can say that a property may have an address without claiming that every
    incomplete graph is wrong. SHACL supplies a use-specific, bounded validation lens: for
    a Property Pack delivery, a shape might require at least one value, a particular
    datatype or membership of a governed concept scheme. A passing report means the data
    met those encoded checks—not that the underlying statement is true.
  </p>
  <p>
    Provenance records explain where a candidate came from, which question it answers,
    which version introduced it, and what review or disposition followed. A prefix or
    source link is not enough: the receipt must identify the actual evidence and its role.
  </p>

  <h2 id="property-pack-walkthrough">Follow one Property Pack construct through the artefacts</h2>
  <p>
    The current machine-proposed Property Pack uses <code>common:Property</code> and
    <code>common:uprn</code> as a concrete worked example. Read the rows together: no
    single file contains the complete agreement, and passing the query or shape does not
    approve the proposed definition or identity criterion.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Property and UPRN across the current Property Pack 0.1 candidate</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Layer</th><th scope="col">What the candidate records</th><th scope="col">Evidence</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">RDF/OWL meaning</th><td><code>common:Property</code> is an OWL class with a proposed definition and identity criterion; <code>common:uprn</code> is an XSD-string datatype property.</td><td><a href="/resource?path=source/03-standards/ontology-candidates/property-pack/0.1/ontology/common.ttl">Common ontology</a></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Semantic home</th><td>Both terms point to the deliberately small <code>common</code> boundary. The proposal remains subject to review.</td><td><a href="/development/property-pack/contexts/common">Common-boundary view</a></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">SKOS value pattern</th><td>The separate response-status scheme demonstrates how governed choices such as Yes, No and Not known remain SKOS concepts rather than subclasses of Property.</td><td><a href="/resource?path=source/03-standards/ontology-candidates/property-pack/0.1/vocabularies/common-controlled.ttl">Common controlled vocabulary</a></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">SHACL expectation</th><td>The Property node shape requires exactly one string UPRN matching digits for this delivery profile.</td><td><a href="/resource?path=source/03-standards/ontology-candidates/property-pack/0.1/shapes/common.ttl">Common shapes graph</a></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Competency query</th><td>An ASK query checks that Property exists as a class and that UPRN has the expected domain and range.</td><td><a href="/resource?path=source/03-standards/ontology-candidates/property-pack/0.1/validation/competency/property-identity.rq">Property identity query</a></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="read-pages">How to read model pages on this site</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Read the page status and candidate version before interpreting the content.</li>
    <li>Use the definition and context to understand intended meaning.</li>
    <li>Read labelled edges as sentences; do not treat layout distance as semantics.</li>
    <li>Inspect tables for datatype properties, controlled values, cardinality and provenance.</li>
    <li>Follow evidence and decision links before treating a machine proposal as an agreement.</li>
    <li>Check whether a result is technical validation, human review or governance promotion.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="callout callout--key">
    <p class="callout__label">Diagrams are selective views</p>
    <p>To remain readable, diagrams show classes and important object relationships. They do not turn literal fields, domain declarations or range declarations into free-standing nodes. Complete technical detail remains in resource tables and downloadable artefacts.</p>
  </div>
  <p><a href="/semantic-modelling/modelling-method">Continue with how SPDTF is modelled →</a></p>
</Layout>
